========================================================================================================================
ITEM 59 -- the cluster temperature / gas-mass power-law index.  Framework: exactly 1/2, no free parameter.
========================================================================================================================
  derivation actually used (so the reader can see where 1/2 comes from and what else rides on it):
    isothermal HSE with rho_gas ~ r^-alpha_g  =>  kT = (mu m_p/alpha_g) * nu(y) g_bar(r) r;  deep-MOND Route A
    nu -> 1/sqrt(y) makes that (mu m_p/alpha_g) sqrt(G M_b a_0), radius-free, slope 1/2, zero point ~ 1/alpha_g.
  eFEDS (Bahar+2022): 446 systems with a measured (non-upper-limit) M_gas and a fitted T
  eRASS1 (Bulbul+2024): 3361 systems with a finite KT and M_gas, z < 1
  median fractional error: eFEDS  T 0.59, M_gas 0.20   |   eRASS1  T 0.67, M_gas 0.24
  -> the eRASS1 all-sky temperatures are 60-70% uncertain per object.  Any slope from the FULL catalogue is a
     statement about the survey's faint end, not about cluster physics.  Both samples are therefore scanned in
     data quality and in mass below, and the scan is reported whether or not it flatters the prediction.

  SCAN A -- eRASS1, slope of log kT on log M_gas as the temperature quality improves:
                         cut      N      r            slope   sigma from 1/2
                  CTS500 > 0   3361   0.61   0.253 +- 0.007            -34.2
                CTS500 > 100   1195   0.73   0.330 +- 0.014            -11.9
                CTS500 > 300    427   0.78   0.361 +- 0.032             -4.3
               CTS500 > 1000    125   0.88   0.419 +- 0.025             -3.2
               CTS500 > 3000     36   0.81   0.422 +- 0.061             -1.3

  SCAN B -- eFEDS (deep field, better spectra), slope as a floor is put on the gas mass:
                         cut      N      r            slope   sigma from 1/2
        M_gas > 1.4e+10 Msun    176   0.69   0.291 +- 0.023             -9.3
        M_gas > 3.5e+12 Msun    132   0.47   0.302 +- 0.045             -4.4
        M_gas > 5.8e+12 Msun    106   0.47   0.378 +- 0.064             -1.9
        M_gas > 1.1e+13 Msun     71   0.50   0.497 +- 0.110             -0.0
        M_gas > 1.4e+13 Msun     53   0.63   0.600 +- 0.116              0.9

  SCAN A/B are POST HOC: the two cuts used below (eRASS1 CTS>1000 and M_gas>1e13; eFEDS T-err<50% and the same
  mass floor) were chosen after looking at those scans, so the headline number is conditional and must be shown
  with its cut dependence, not quoted alone.  The full grid, eRASS1:
                   M_gas>0e+00   M_gas>3e+12   M_gas>1e+13   M_gas>3e+13
         CTS>300    0.36+-0.03    0.45+-0.02    0.46+-0.03    0.45+-0.07
         CTS>600    0.37+-0.04    0.46+-0.02    0.45+-0.03    0.51+-0.07
        CTS>1000    0.42+-0.02    0.51+-0.02    0.49+-0.04    0.55+-0.07
        CTS>2000    0.42+-0.05    0.53+-0.03    0.48+-0.09    0.59+-0.11
  the grid moves between about 0.36 and 0.55 -- the 1/2 is not knife-edge on the cut, but it is not pinned to
  +-0.03 by these data either, and the eFEDS confirmation below is a genuinely independent survey, not a re-cut.

  eRASS1 CTS>1000, M_gas>1e13:  N = 91, log M_gas spread 0.32 dex, correlation 0.79
      OLS  kT | M_gas   (the item's literal ask)     0.493 +- 0.042   (  -0.2 sigma from 1/2)
      1/OLS M_gas | kT  (relation read backwards)    0.782 +- 0.062   (  +4.5 sigma from 1/2)
      orthogonal / TLS                               0.559 +- 0.039   (  +1.5 sigma from 1/2)
      EIV with quoted errors, scatter in kT          0.511 +- 0.045   (  +0.2 sigma from 1/2)
      intrinsic scatter in log kT at fixed M_gas: 0.064 +- 0.018 dex

  eFEDS T-err<50%, M_gas>1e13:  N = 74, log M_gas spread 0.21 dex, correlation 0.51
      OLS  kT | M_gas   (the item's literal ask)     0.507 +- 0.103   (  +0.1 sigma from 1/2)
      1/OLS M_gas | kT  (relation read backwards)    1.935 +- 1.029   (  +1.4 sigma from 1/2)
      orthogonal / TLS                               0.982 +- 0.440   (  +1.1 sigma from 1/2)
      EIV with quoted errors, scatter in kT          0.513 +- 0.082   (  +0.2 sigma from 1/2)
      intrinsic scatter in log kT at fixed M_gas: 0.097 +- 0.052 dex

  MUTATION CONTROL 1 -- estimator bias.  Mock catalogues built with a TRUE slope of exactly 1/2, the observed
  log M_gas spread, and the observed per-object errors; each estimator is then run on the mock.  An estimator that
  does not return 0.50 here cannot be used to test 0.50 on the data.
      OLS kT|M_gas       recovers 0.498 +- 0.055 from an injected 0.500  (bias -0.002)
      1/OLS M_gas|kT     recovers 0.932 +- 0.116 from an injected 0.500  (bias +0.432)
      orthogonal         recovers 0.599 +- 0.065 from an injected 0.500  (bias +0.099)
      EIV                recovers 0.498 +- 0.043 from an injected 0.500  (bias -0.002)
  [PASS] M1 mutation control -- with the real error budget the forward regression and the EIV fit are nearly unbiased on an injected 1/2, while the inverse regression is biased HIGH by ~0.2; so the forward/EIV numbers are the ones that may be compared with 1/2 and the inverse number may not   (OLS kT|M_gas bias -0.002, inverse bias +0.432, orthogonal bias +0.099)
  [PASS] M2 mutation control -- shuffling the gas masses destroys the relation (slope consistent with zero) and an injected 0.30 slope is recovered to 1%, so the estimator is measuring the relation and not a normalisation artefact   (shuffled slope -0.0057 +- 0.0634; injected 0.300 recovered as 0.3000)

  the two independent samples, cluster regime, forward regression:  eRASS1 0.493 +- 0.042 (N=91),  eFEDS 0.507 +- 0.103 (N=74)
  full-range value, groups included:  eRASS1 all counts 0.253 +- 0.007,  eFEDS all masses 0.291 +- 0.023

  is the low-mass flattening a resolved BEND, or a level offset at the faint end?  Quadratic fit of log kT on
  log M_gas; the framework and self-similar LambdaCDM both require a pure power law, i.e. zero curvature:
      eRASS1 all         N= 3361: d2 log kT/d(log M_gas)^2 = +0.0647 +- 0.0053 (+12.3 sigma from a pure power law)
      eRASS1 CTS>300     N=  427: d2 log kT/d(log M_gas)^2 = +0.0741 +- 0.0126 (+5.9 sigma from a pure power law)
      eRASS1 CTS>1000    N=  125: d2 log kT/d(log M_gas)^2 = +0.0525 +- 0.0380 (+1.4 sigma from a pure power law)
      eFEDS T-err<50%    N=  177: d2 log kT/d(log M_gas)^2 = +0.0300 +- 0.0246 (+1.2 sigma from a pure power law)
  [PASS] 59 curvature AGAINST BOTH SIDES -- the eRASS1 relation is NOT a pure power law: the quadratic term is +0.065 +- 0.005 over the full catalogue and +0.074 +- 0.013 at CTS>300, so the T-M_gas relation steepens with mass.  Neither the framework's exact 1/2 nor self-similar LambdaCDM allows any curvature, so whichever index is quoted is a local tangent, not a law.  Stated against the finding as well: the significance falls to 1.4 sigma in the deepest cut and to 1.2 sigma in eFEDS, so a residual data-quality gradient is NOT excluded as the cause   (eRASS1 all: +0.0647 +- 0.0053; eRASS1 CTS>300: +0.0741 +- 0.0126; eRASS1 CTS>1000: +0.0525 +- 0.0380; eFEDS T-err<50%: +0.0300 +- 0.0246)

  the LambdaCDM alternative, computed here and not quoted: self-similar kT ~ (M500 E(z))^{2/3} plus an
  f_gas-M500 slope s gives d log kT/d log M_gas = (2/3)/(1+s).  First the slope s measured inside each sample:
      eRASS1 CTS>1000, M_gas>1e13      f_gas slope s = +0.317 +- 0.036; measured d log T/d log M500 = 0.621 +- 0.063 (self-similar 0.667); chain -> 0.506
      eFEDS T-err<50%, M_gas>1e13      f_gas slope s = -0.025 +- 0.064; measured d log T/d log M500 = 0.561 +- 0.116 (self-similar 0.667); chain -> 0.683
      those two internal values DISAGREE (s = +0.32 vs -0.03) and neither may be trusted: in both catalogues the
      M500 and the M_gas are built from the same count rate and R500 is derived from M500, so f_gas-vs-M500 inside
      one of these samples is largely a calibration relation.  That is exactly the confound hunt item 19 recorded.
      So the LambdaCDM chain is instead evaluated over the EXTERNAL range the item itself quotes for f_gas slopes
      from hydro simulations and from item 19, s = 0.20 to 0.40:
        s = 0.40 -> 0.476;   s = 0.30 -> 0.513;   s = 0.20 -> 0.556
  [PASS] 59 RESULT -- in the cluster regime with well-measured temperatures the index IS 1/2 in two independent eROSITA samples, but it is NOT a discriminating hit: the LambdaCDM chain (self-similar T plus an f_gas slope in the range 0.20-0.40) covers 0.476-0.556 and is indistinguishable from 1/2 at this precision, exactly as the item itself warned   (eRASS1 0.493 +- 0.042 (N=91, -0.2 sigma from 1/2), eFEDS 0.507 +- 0.103 (N=74, +0.1 sigma); LambdaCDM chain 0.476-0.556)
  [PASS] 59 AGAINST INTEREST -- the SAME relation extended down into the group regime is NOT 1/2: the full-range forward slope is 0.25 +- 0.01 (eRASS1, all counts, N=3361) and 0.29 +- 0.02 (eFEDS, all masses, N=176), 9-34 sigma below the prediction, and the framework has no escape there because groups sit DEEPER in the deep-MOND regime, where the 1/2 should hold best   (eRASS1 full 0.253 +- 0.007 (-34 sigma), eFEDS full 0.291 +- 0.023 (-9 sigma))
  the honest reading of that split, both ways: the slope climbs MONOTONICALLY with data quality (eRASS1 0.25 at all
  counts -> 0.42 at CTS>1000) and with mass floor (eFEDS 0.29 -> 0.50), which is the signature of a low-count
  temperature bias plus X-ray flux selection at the faint end, not necessarily of physics.  It is NOT possible to
  separate those from these catalogues, so the group-regime miss is logged as an OPEN systematic on both sides
  (LambdaCDM's self-similar 2/3 fails there by even more) and NOT as a kill of the framework.

  zero point.  kT = (mu m_p/alpha_g) sqrt(G M_b a_0) has ONE O(1) unknown, the outer gas log-slope alpha_g.
  Solving for it at the sample median is the only honest use of the normalisation.  Baryons M_b = M_gas + M_star
  with the repo's standard stellar prescription f_star = clip(0.025 (M500/1e14)^-0.3, 0.01, 0.08).
      eRASS1 CTS>1000, M_gas>1e13      canonical  implied alpha_g = 1.18;  at the canonical alpha_g = 2 that is a further mass factor eta_T = 2.86;  mass-side eta at R500 on the same rows = 1.99
      eRASS1 CTS>1000, M_gas>1e13      alt        implied alpha_g = 1.30;  at the canonical alpha_g = 2 that is a further mass factor eta_T = 2.37;  mass-side eta at R500 on the same rows = 1.82
      eFEDS T-err<50%, M_gas>1e13      canonical  implied alpha_g = 1.24;  at the canonical alpha_g = 2 that is a further mass factor eta_T = 2.60;  mass-side eta at R500 on the same rows = 2.27
      eFEDS T-err<50%, M_gas>1e13      alt        implied alpha_g = 1.36;  at the canonical alpha_g = 2 that is a further mass factor eta_T = 2.16;  mass-side eta at R500 on the same rows = 2.08
      the Newtonian/LambdaCDM version of the same line, for comparison: kT = (mu m_p/alpha_g) G M500/R500 gives
      eRASS1 CTS>1000, M_gas>1e13      LambdaCDM  implied alpha_g = 2.77 -- also O(1), also unconstrained
      the one non-trivial thing the zero point does say: the temperature side and the mass side must be reconciled
      by the SAME missing factor.  Applying the mass-side eta measured on these very rows to the temperature
      normalisation gives alpha_g = 1.67 (canonical) / 1.75 (alt) -- inside the physical 1.5-2.5 band for the
      outer gas log-slope.  That is internal bookkeeping consistency, NOT evidence: the residual eta itself is the
      unexplained part, and it is the same cluster residual the rest of the repo carries.
  [PASS] 59 zero point AGAINST INTEREST -- the a_0 normalisation is NOT a test.  It buys exactly one number, the outer gas log-slope alpha_g, which comes out 1.18-1.36 on the two footings against a physical 1.5-2.5; read as a mass boost at alpha_g = 2 that is a cluster residual eta_T = 2.2-2.9, some 25-50% larger than the mass-side eta = 1.8-2.0 measured on the same rows.  The Newtonian version needs an equally free alpha_g = 2.8, so the zero point falsifies neither side   (canonical alpha_g = 1.18 (eta_T = 2.86, mass-side eta = 1.99), alt alpha_g = 1.30 (eta_T = 2.37, mass-side eta = 1.82); reconciled alpha_g = 1.67/1.75)
  [PASS] M3 mutation control -- multiplying a_0 by ten moves the implied alpha_g by exactly sqrt(10), so the zero point really is carrying a_0 and is not insensitive to it   (alpha_g(a_0) = 1.182 -> alpha_g(10 a_0) = 3.738, ratio 3.1623 vs sqrt(10) = 3.1623)

